    "published": null,
    "text": "Here are the relevant news articles:\n\n**US Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion, Threatening Global Economy and Israel**\nAccording to a report by the 'Jerusalem Post' cited by 'Sada al-Balad', US public debt has surpassed $40 trillion, creating a 'massive financial hole' that threatens to halt the global wave of interest rate cuts and impose heavy costs on other economies, including Israel. The US debt is growing at a rate of $7 billion daily, with net interest payments reaching approximately $970 billion in fiscal year 2025; the Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects these payments will exceed $1 trillion for the first time this year, surpassing total defense spending, and could double to $2.1 trillion by 2036, representing a quarter of the federal budget. The International Monetary Fund (IMF) estimates US debt will exceed 140% of GDP by 2031. To finance the deficit, the US administration issued bonds worth $18.9 trillion from the start of 2026 through July, a 10.2% increase over the previous year, which drains global liquidity and pushes bond yields higher. Israel is already paying a price through three channels: higher interest rates increasing mortgage and loan installments; volatility on Wall Street increasing risks for Israeli retirement savings; and budget pressure forcing the state to spend billions of shekels on debt interest at the expense of education, health, and security. In a preventive measure, the regulatory body mandated financial institutions to hold a 10% foreign currency liquidity reserve. Experts like Jay Breminger of PricewaterhouseCoopers Israel warn that the technology and real estate sectors are most vulnerable, as high-risk assets lose funding when US Treasury bonds offer low-risk returns of 5%. Paul Marino of 'Themes ETFs' notes the dollar's reserve status gives the US a 'higher credit line,' while analyst Violeta Todorova of 'Leverage Shares' warns the risk lies in financing costs rather than default, as reduced foreign absorption could force the Treasury to raise yields in a vicious cycle. The Treasury has already doubled long-term bond buybacks from $2 billion to $4 billion per operation to curb yields, which reached 4.7% for 10-year bonds and 5.2% for 30-year bonds. Michael Peterson, CEO of the 'Peter G. Peterson Foundation', stated the US has run a deficit for 26 years, and every dollar paid in interest reduces citizen services and increases the likelihood of tax hikes. The report concludes that the current intervention is temporary and the final bill for the $40 trillion debt will be paid by consumers and companies worldwide through eroded wages, lower savings, and higher taxes.\nOriginal language: ar\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 12:54 PM\nSource:[صدى البلد](https://www.elbalad.news/7105671)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**Economist Juan Ramón Rallo Warns of Fiscal Crisis in US, France, and Japan as Interest Rates Soar**\nIn a podcast episode published on September 12, 2026, economist Juan Ramón Rallo criticizes the fiscal policies of the United States, France, and Japan, arguing that governments are increasing deficits despite soaring interest rates. Rallo notes that 10-year US Treasury yields have reached 5%, French yields are at 4.5%, and Japanese yields are at 3%, levels not seen in decades. He highlights that interest payments are exploding: US federal interest costs are projected to rise from $970 billion to $1.1 trillion (a 15% increase) by 2026; French interest payments are set to jump from €51.6 billion to €64 billion (a 24% increase); and Japan's interest burden is expected to grow from ¥10.5 trillion to over ¥13 trillion (a 25% increase). Rallo warns that the situation will worsen in 2027, with interest costs projected to rise another 20-25% in each country. He specifically criticizes Donald Trump's proposed 'Trump Dividend' of a $5,000 check per adult, which would cost $1.3 trillion (4% of US GDP), adding to a federal debt that has already surpassed $40 trillion. In France, the Prime Minister admitted the government cannot meet its 5% deficit target for 2026, with the Fitch agency projecting a 5.2% deficit this year and 5.5% next. Meanwhile, Japan has announced a record budget of ¥143 trillion for 2027, a 17% increase, while simultaneously cutting the consumption tax on food from 8% to 1%, a move that reduces revenue by ¥5 trillion annually. Rallo concludes that this 'fiscal suicide' will lead to higher inflation and interest rates, ultimately impoverishing citizens.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 07:33 PM\nSource:[Podcast de Juan Ramón Rallo](https://sphinx.acast.com/p/open/s/62754272a1fc7500122b7822/e/6aa5a8f8564171e32ab47188/media.mp3)\n\n**The Age of the Fed Is Over as Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion**\nAccording to an article published on September 12, 2026, by MV Financial (a Washington DC-area asset manager), the era of Federal Reserve dominance is ending as the Federal Open Market Committee prepares for a meeting. The article notes that outstanding US government debt has surpassed $40 trillion, making interest payments the second-largest budget line item. Despite the Federal Reserve not changing its funds rate, the bond market has set longer-term rates independently, with yields rising over 0.5% this year. The text argues that high inflation, massive debt, and global disruptions are sustaining elevated rates beyond the Fed's direct control. Consequently, the Federal Reserve must adapt quickly to these market realities to avoid losing credibility, as its traditional rate management tools now have less impact on broader yields.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 03:35 AM\nSource:[Seeking Alpha](https://seekingalpha.com/article/4945720-age-of-fed-is-over)\n\n**US Budget Deficit Hits $1.97 Trillion in First 11 Months of Fiscal Year**\nThe U.S. Treasury Department announced that the federal budget deficit for the first 11 months of the fiscal year (ending in August) stood at $1.97 trillion, surpassing the total deficit of $1.775 trillion recorded for the full fiscal year 2025. In August alone, the unadjusted deficit fell by 52% to $167 billion, down from the previous year, according to the BBC. This decline was driven by reduced spending, including refunds for illegally collected tariffs, a rare drop in interest payments, and shifts in benefit disbursement dates. However, when adjusting for timing shifts that moved some Medicare and other benefit payments from August to July, the August deficit was $248 billion, representing a $7 billion increase compared to August of the previous year. Unadjusted federal spending in August dropped 24% to $527 billion, with interest payments falling by $14 billion for the month due to changes in inflation adjustment calculations. Conversely, cumulative interest payments since the start of the fiscal year rose by $143 billion (13%) compared to the same period last year.\nOriginal language: ar\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 02:38 AM\nSource:[بوابة فيتو](https://www.vetogate.com/5730002)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**US GDP Growth Holds at 1.5% in Q2 2026 as Fed Maintains Rates and Debt Rises**\nThe US economy grew by 1.5% in the second quarter of 2026 compared to the previous year, according to data from the Bureau of Economic Analysis under the US Department of Commerce, maintaining the rate from the first estimate. On June 17, the Federal Reserve (Fed) kept the key interest rate unchanged within the target range of 3.5-3.75% per annum, citing that inflation remains above the 2% target. Additionally, US government debt rose by $0.24 trillion in May to reach $39.2 trillion, according to the US Department of the Treasury. At the end of the fiscal year 2025 (ending September 2025), debt stood at 124% of GDP, and the IMF projects it will rise to 142% of GDP by 2031. The average debt servicing rate was 3.35% at the end of May, having increased from 1.6% in 2021.\nOriginal language: ru\nPublish date: August 26, 2026 12:00 AM\nSource:[Ведомости](https://www.vedomosti.ru/politics/news/2026/08/26/1223886-ssha-sohranili-otsenku)\n\n**US Public Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion Amid Rising Interest Rates and Fiscal Concerns**\nOn August 19, 2026, the public debt of the United States surpassed 40 trillion dollars (specifically $40,470,470,230,213), having more than doubled in value between 2016 and 2026, with a recent annual increase of 3 trillion dollars. According to market data, the 10-year Treasury bond rate reached 4.71 percent, the highest since 2007, while the 30-year rate hit 5.26 percent, its highest level in 19 years. The International Bourse (BIS) states that the probability of fiscal consolidation is nearly zero, noting that historical market manias often follow technological booms with capital excess, eventually leading to investment reversals and recessions. Currently, 80 percent of the debt is held by domestic agents including the Federal Reserve, foreign governments, pension funds, and private investors, while the remaining 20 percent is owed to federal agencies like Social Security. Approximately one-quarter of the debt is held by international investors and foreign governments such as Japan, the United Kingdom, and China. The estimated interest payment for this fiscal year exceeds 1 trillion dollars at an average rate of 3.5 percent, with a daily interest cost of approximately 3.3 billion dollars, resulting in a debt-to-GDP ratio of 123 percent. The article notes that aggressive fiscal policies and the economic impact of the war in Iran and rising oil prices have contributed to this scenario, while a recent attempt by the Treasury to buy back long-term bonds to lower yields failed to achieve its objective.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: August 24, 2026 12:00 AM\nSource:[La Jornada](https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/08/24/opinion/deuda-publica-y-el-dolar)\n\n**US National Debt Approaches $40 Trillion Amid Silent Default Concerns**\nAs of July 30, 2026, the US federal debt stood at $39.84 trillion, just $159 billion away from the symbolic $40 trillion milestone, according to Treasury data. If the debt continues to grow at the average pace of the previous month, it is projected to surpass $40 trillion by the end of August. This swelling debt burden, coupled with rising borrowing costs and interest payments, is exerting pressure on government finances. The article highlights a debate in financial circles regarding a 'silent default', defined as a situation where a government does not fail to repay creditors but allows inflation to erode the real value of its debt. Unlike a sovereign default, which is considered highly unlikely for the US due to its control over the dollar and deep bond markets, the risk of a silent default is seen as more subtle and plausible. The Congressional Budget Office (CBO) estimated in February that net federal interest payments would exceed $1 trillion in fiscal year 2026 (3.3% of GDP) and could reach $2.1 trillion by 2036 (4.6% of GDP). Additionally, debt held by the public is projected to rise from 101% of GDP in 2026 to 120% by 2036, surpassing the 1946 post-war peak. With core inflation near 3% and the Federal Reserve's benchmark rate unchanged at 3.5% to 3.75% as of July, the real return on Treasury securities remains limited. The article suggests that while an outright default is remote, the erosion of debt value could be reinforced by continued reliance on short-term borrowing, regulatory incentives, and measures like higher tariffs. The consequences could include reduced fiscal space for domestic spending and a potential long-term decline in the dollar's share of global reserves.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: August 16, 2026 09:31 PM\nSource:[The Express Tribune](https://tribune.com.pk/story/2624265/is-us-drifting-towards-silent-default)\n\n**US Treasury Issues $25 Billion in Bonds at 25-Year High Interest Rate of 5.216%**\nAccording to data from the US Treasury Department, the US federal debt reached approximately $39.77 trillion by the end of July 2026, with projections indicating it could rise to 120% of GDP by 2036, according to the Congressional Budget Office. In a significant development, the Treasury Department issued $25 billion in 30-year bonds on August 14, 2026, where investors demanded an interest rate of 5.216%, marking the highest level in 25 years since August 2001. This rate represents a sharp increase from 5.06% in the previous sale and 4.91% prior to the start of Donald Trump's second term. The article notes that while the bid-to-cover ratio was 2.39, the primary concern for investors is the cost of borrowing rather than the ability to sell debt. Consequently, annual interest costs on the federal debt are expected to exceed $1 trillion, accounting for nearly one-fifth of total federal spending. The Congressional Budget Office further projects that the federal budget deficit will widen from $1.9 trillion to $3.1 trillion by 2036, with net interest payments rising to 4.6% of GDP. The Treasury Department plans to maintain current long-term issuance structures through October 2026, relying on Treasury bills to meet short-term financing needs.\nOriginal language: tr\nPublish date: August 14, 2026 11:23 AM\nSource:[Dünya](https://www.dunya.com/ekonomi/abd-borcuna-25-yilin-en-agir-faturasi-haberi-836231)\n\n**US Daily Interest Payments Surpass $3 Billion as Debt Costs Soar**\nAccording to calculations by the US Congressional Budget Office (CBO), the US federal government spent approximately $963 billion in net interest on public debt between October and July of the 2026 fiscal year, a figure that is $117 billion higher than the same period last year, representing a 14% year-over-year increase. Consequently, the daily average net interest expense for the US Treasury has exceeded $3 billion, bringing the total interest payments for the fiscal year close to the $1 trillion mark before the year ends. Additionally, the US federal budget deficit reached approximately $1.8 trillion in the first 10 months of the fiscal year, an increase of $169 billion compared to the previous year. While federal revenues grew by approximately 3% annually, expenditures rose by 5%, creating a cycle where high deficits necessitate more borrowing, which in turn increases interest costs. The article notes that even if market interest rates fall, the budget will not see immediate relief because a large portion of the debt stock must be refinanced at higher rates over time, squeezing the US government's ability to fund other areas.\nOriginal language: tr\nPublish date: August 11, 2026 11:43 AM\nSource:[Dünya](https://www.dunya.com/ekonomi/abd-her-gun-3-milyar-dolari-faize-oduyor-haberi-835783)\n\n**US Interest Payments Surpass Defense Budget in Fiscal Year 2025**\nAccording to a new report from the Government Accountability Office (GAO), the U.S. federal government spent over $970 billion on interest payments in fiscal year 2025, an amount exceeding the budget for national defense and nearly matching Medicare spending. This interest cost represented 3.2 percent of GDP, with public debt reaching $30.2 trillion, or 99 percent of GDP, by the end of the fiscal year. The GAO projects that debt held by the public will hit a historical record of 106 percent of GDP by 2029 and could reach 251 percent of GDP by 2056 if current policies remain unchanged. Jordan Haring of the American Action Forum highlighted a widening gap where spending is projected to climb to nearly 35 percent of GDP by 2056 against revenue sitting at 17.1 percent. The Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget warned that rising debt and interest costs create a reinforcing cycle that becomes harder to reverse over time. Projections indicate that by 2044, net interest spending will surpass Social Security as the largest category of federal spending. The GAO further warns that high debt levels could increase interest rates for households and businesses, slow economic growth, and reduce the government's ability to respond to future crises.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: June 23, 2026 07:50 AM\nSource:[Redstate](https://redstate.com/ben-smith/2026/06/23/washington-spent-970-billion-on-interest-last-year-thats-more-than-defense-n2203617)\n\n**30-Year Treasury Yields Hit 5%: Debt Service Costs Soar to $1.2 Trillion Amid Oil Shock**\nThe yield on the 30-year US Treasury bond has retaken the 5% mark, with long-end yields trading at 4.99% intraday on Tuesday, just 8 basis points from an 18-year high, while the S&P 500 remains near record territory. This divergence is driven by energy prices, as oil has risen more than 50% since late February to above $100 a barrel following conflict in the Strait of Hormuz. Bank of America strategist Michael Hartnett labels the 5% level the 'bond market’s Maginot Line,' warning that a breach could signal the end of a risk asset bull regime. The fiscal impact is severe: according to the Bureau of Economic Analysis, federal interest payments reached an annualized $1.23 trillion in Q1 2026 (4.18% of GDP), a figure the Government Accountability Office confirmed crossed $1.2 trillion in fiscal 2025. With the national debt past $39 trillion and roughly one-third of public debt rolling over annually, the Congressional Budget Office projects net interest payments will total $16.2 trillion over the next decade. Consequently, the average 30-year mortgage rate has climbed above 6.4%, according to Zillow, while Oxford Economics analyst John Canavan predicts the next rate cut timing may be pushed back from June to December due to persistent inflation.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: May 05, 2026 08:39 PM\nSource:[Benzinga](https://www.benzinga.com/analyst-stock-ratings/analyst-color/26/05/52305267/30-year-treasury-yield-5-percent-trump-interest-expense-1-2-trillion-gdp)\n\n**US Debt Surpasses 100% of GDP, Approaching WWII Record**\nAccording to a Wall Street Journal report, the US public debt exceeded 100% of GDP for the first time since 1946, reaching a ratio of 100.2% as of March 31, 2026. The US government debt stood at $31.27 trillion, while the GDP for 2025 was $31.22 trillion, compared to 99.5% at the end of the previous fiscal year on September 30. The Wall Street Journal notes that the federal deficit is nearly 6% of GDP, with a projected deficit of $1.9 trillion for the current year, similar to 2025 levels. The final figure depends on war spending with Iran, tariff returns, and economic conditions. The article states that while the 100% threshold itself is not critical, it symbolizes long-term financial pressure; currently, one in every seven dollars of federal spending goes to interest payments, and a 0.1% interest rate hike would cost the US $379 billion over 10 years. The US Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects the debt-to-GDP ratio will reach 100.6% this fiscal year and exceed the post-World War II record by 2030. Unlike the 2020 surge caused by the pandemic and recession, current deficits are described as structural rather than temporary, with higher interest rates.\nOriginal language: ru\nPublish date: May 02, 2026 09:00 AM\nSource:[Ukrrudprom](https://ukrrudprom.com/news/Gosdolg_SSHA_previsil_VVP_i_pribligaetsya_k_urovnyam_Vtoroy_mirovoy.html)\n\n"
